### Transparent onset rule

Implement a configurable experimental rainfall-based onset rule. For example, a candidate onset may be flagged after at least 20 mm of rainfall over a rolling 3-day period.

Treat this only as an initial experimental rule. It is not an official IMD declaration.

The methodology must distinguish:
- A rainfall threshold being met.
- A candidate onset being identified.
- Persistence being confirmed using subsequent rainfall.
- An official monsoon onset declared by an authorised meteorological source.

Do not claim that future rainfall persistence is already confirmed when it has not yet occurred.

### Break and dry-spell rules

Use configurable definitions for:
- Dry day.
- Consecutive dry days.
- Prolonged dry spell.
- Candidate monsoon break.
- Rainfall deficit relative to historical climatology.

An initial dry-day threshold may be rainfall below 2.5 mm, with a prolonged dry spell threshold of more than 7 consecutive dry days. These are experimental starting points and must be configurable and locally validated.

Do not call every dry week a confirmed meteorological monsoon break.

